{"Reasoning": "This is a life expectancy question conditioned on having already lived 10 years. I will use publicly available life expectancy data. The CDC provides life expectancy data for the US. For a male born in the US, the life expectancy at birth is around 73-77 years (this fluctuates slightly year to year). However, the question asks for a male who 'has lived at least 10 years'. This means we are looking for the conditional life expectancy of a 10-year-old male. People who have already survived early childhood illnesses and accidents have a higher remaining life expectancy than at birth. According to actuarial tables and life expectancy calculators (e.g., Social Security Administration life tables, CDC life tables for the US), the life expectancy for a 10-year-old male in the US is generally in the range of 66-68 additional years. Adding these 66-68 years to the current age of 10 gives an expected age at death of 76-78 years old. I will take the midpoint or a slightly conservative estimate. Therefore, 77.5 years seems like a reasonable estimate.", "Answer": "77.5", "Confidence": "0.9"}
```

