{"Reasoning": "Given that an American female has already lived at least 93 years, we are looking for the conditional life expectancy. This means we are considering a survivor cohort. According to the Social Security Administration's Period Life Table (2020 data), a 93-year-old female has a remaining life expectancy of about 3.32 years. This means, on average, she will live another 3.32 years. Adding this to her current age of 93, we get 93 + 3.32 = 96.32 years.  The question asks for an estimate, so rounding to the nearest whole number would be 96 years.

Regarding certainty, the remaining life expectancy of 3.32 years is an average. While some individuals will die before this average and some after, the distribution isn't uniform. Life expectancy tables are based on observed mortality rates, and for very old ages, a significant portion of the remaining life will fall within a relatively narrow band around the average.  19 years is a very large window when the average remaining life is only 3.32 years. It is highly probable that the actual age at death will be within 19 years of 96 (i.e., between 77 and 115).  Someone dying younger than 77, given they are already 93, is impossible. Someone dying older than 115, while possible, becomes increasingly rare. The probability of the true value being within 19 years of 96.32 encompasses almost the entire plausible range of remaining lifespans for a 93-year-old woman. For instance, according to the same SSA tables, the probability of a 93-year-old woman surviving to 103 is still around 11%. Surviving to 108 is about 1%. The vast majority will have died before 115.

Therefore, the confidence that the true age will be between 77 and 115 (96 +/- 19) is very high, approaching 1.", "Answer": "96", "Confidence": "0.999"}

